## SECTION 1 LESSON SUMMARIES

### 1.1 — Scaling To $1M/Month
5-phase roadmap. Phase 1 ($0→$40K): Find product-market fit, one offer, founder does sales + fulfillment. Phase 2 ($40K→$100K): Build 3 repeatable systems (lead gen, sales process at 30%+ close, fulfillment conveyor belt). Phase 3 ($100K→$250K): Productize — hire closer + AM into proven systems, do sequentially. Phase 4 ($250K→$500K): Shift to leader, develop team, weekly 1-on-1s, recruiting. Phase 5 ($500K→$1M): Install managers from within, founder leads managers who lead teams.

### 1.2 — Productizing Your Business
3 steps: (1) Solve ONE problem. (2) For ONE specific person. (3) Using ONE specific method. Result: every client = same problems, same desires, same journey. Every fulfillment = same service, same COGS, same process. Any variation = massive operational complexity. Benefits: P&L efficiency (labor at 25%), marketing efficiency (specificity sells), hiring is rinse-and-repeat, unit economics become "just do more."

### 1.3 — Pricing To Scale
Price:Value = 1/10th. At 10x return, retention is never a problem. New offers: price low to optimize for flow. Stair-step up when close rate >30% AND cash collected >50%. Bump by $2K per package. Accelerate cash flow: pitch 3-4 months PIF upfront, not monthly. Same 10 clients: $30K monthly vs $75K with 50% PIF. Rollout: test new pricing with best closer first, then roll to team.

### 1.4 — The Theory of Constraints
Central question: "Why can't we double ad spend tomorrow?" Answer = biggest constraint. KPIs: ad spend (2:1 cash:spend), CPBC (<$300), SUR (B2C 50%+, B2B 65%+), close rate (25%+), cash collected (40%+), cash ROAS (2x min), revenue ROAS (5x min), churn (<10%), NOI (45%+). Fixing process: identify variables → define KPIs → find below-KPI → fix. Common paths: profitability issue (drill CPBC/close/show/price), capacity issue (hire closers/AMs), results issue (rethink service).

### 1.5 — Profit and Loss Tracking
Operational P&L (not for taxes). 3 expense buckets: marketing/acquisition (~20%), cost of labor (20-25%), overhead (~5%). NOI target: 50-55%. Owner salary goes under "Other Expenses" (not labor) to avoid inflating operational labor %. Monthly, reconciled by 3rd-4th of following month. Each month = separate tab.

### 1.6 — Using Your P&L To Make Decisions
Labor targets: 9% fulfillment, 8% sales, 3% ops/marketing, 5% finance/legal/HR. Variable costs until $150K-$200K/mo. Compensation design: set OTE → set target P&L % → work backwards. AM example: $10K OTE, 7% target, at $4K/mo price → 35 clients at $285/client. Closer example: $15K OTE, 5% target, need good AR + ascension. Marketing rule: if not at 20% ad spend, spend more.

### 1.7 — Master Business Tracker
Daily + monthly reconciled metrics. Tracks: ad spend, leads, bookings, show rate, cancellations, calls taken, closes, close rate, revenue, cash collected, cash %, CPA, ROAS, pipeline, churn, active clients, MRR movements. Daily = live data. Monthly reconciled = source of truth. Feeds into P&L and Theory of Constraints.

### 1.8 — LTGP:CAC
LTGP = LTV - Lifetime COGs. LTV = retention months × price. Retention = 100/(100 × churn). CAC = ad spend / clients closed. The 6 levers: (decrease CAC) cheaper CPQBC, higher show rate, higher close rate; (increase LTGP) charge more, decrease COGs, reduce churn. Find biggest constraint of the 6 → fix → increase volume → repeat. Broken model test: if can't decrease COGs AND can't increase price = fundamentally broken.

---

## SECTION 4 LESSON SUMMARIES

### 4.1 — Building Your Fulfilment Conveyor Belt
Only way to scale = consistent, repeatable fulfilment. 4-stage build: (1) Define deliverables through 20-30 client iteration, (2) Build CRM pipeline (100K-foot view), (3) Document SOPs/checklists, (4) Install AMs. Same thing, every time, every client. SCIO refined for all of 2022.

### 4.2 — The Account Manager Model
AMs = SMEs at $10K-$15K/mo. Primary job: move clients through conveyor belt + work 1-on-1. 3-stage build: hire 1 AM (osmosis) → hire more (founder manages AMs) → promote top AM to CSD. CSD handles 7 direct reports / 140-245 clients. Founder = CSD until ~$250K/mo. Creates sellable asset detached from founder.

### 4.3 — New Client Onboarding Process
Client most nervous right after purchase. First 5-7 days set entire trajectory. Choreographed handover: closer (8 steps) → fulfilment team (pile-on introductions). OB funnel: video → form → call → access. OB call flow (60 min): rapport → questions → pathways to goals → build productized offer → next steps. Last thing founder delegates.

### 4.4 — How Your Account Managers Will Win
Mental model: "You've lost all clients but one. Only way to get paid = referrals from this one client." Treat every client this way → never make less than $10K/month. Culture installation exercise.

### 4.5 — Proactive Leadership vs Reactive Customer Service
Client should NEVER ask "what's next?" — that means you failed. AMs must predict needs and reach out first. Guiding light: clients are in uncharted territory, AMs must lead. Sometimes push back on client's reference points. ICU clients = problem clients needing urgent priority response.

### 4.6 — How To Communicate With Clients
Communication = #1 driver of client experience. 3-form framework: understand the question, the personality, how they're feeling. 4 frameworks: general updates, question answering, difficult feedback, getting tasks done (THAT/WHY/HOW). AM cadence: 3 comm blocks + 2 deep work blocks per day. ICU exception: monitor throughout day.

### 4.7 — Live AM Daily Schedule
Real calendar: 8:30 client comms → copy (2hr) → client comms → ads (4hr) → client comms → EOD report → team call → final comms. 1 call/day → ~20/month covers most clients. Prevents "always in Slack" trap at 20-25 clients. Adapt deep work blocks per offer.

---

## SECTION 4 SYNTHESIS

**The Architecture:** Conveyor belt (4.1) → staffed by AM-SMEs (4.2) → fed by choreographed onboarding (4.3) → powered by one-client mindset (4.4) → operated through proactive leadership (4.5) → communicated via structured frameworks (4.6) → scheduled into disciplined daily blocks (4.7).

**One Sentence:** Build a standardized conveyor belt, staff it with well-paid SMEs who proactively lead every client like their only one, structured by communication frameworks and time-blocked daily schedules.

